RDL_TECHNIQUE_PROTOCOL_EN = """
Romanian deadlift (RDL) – TECHNIQUE COACHING
Activate this protocol when the user mentions an RDL, Romanian deadlift, hip hinge, or difficulty controlling spinal position.

COACHING APPROACH
- First clarify whether the issue is pain/discomfort or simply learning the movement. If pain has not been mentioned, do not assume it is absent: ask briefly and directly. If pain is present, do not prescribe continuing the loaded movement; follow the safety rules.
- For a technique-only question, do not say the movement happens only at the hips. Explain that the pelvis moves backwards as the hips flex, the torso inclines forward, and the spine maintains a controlled, natural position. Do not demand excessive rigidity or a forcibly flattened back.
- Do not promise that the spine is completely motionless. The aim is a controlled position without unwanted rounding or excessive arching under load.
- Do not default to a resistance band looped around the feet or behind the body as a teaching aid. Avoid improvised setups that are unnecessary or confusing.

LEARNING SEQUENCE (easier to more demanding)
1. Wall hip hinge: stand with your back to a wall, initially about 10–20 cm away, knees slightly bent. Move the pelvis backwards until the glutes gently touch the wall. Move closer if needed; increase distance gradually when easy. Do not force range.
2. If useful, add a dowel along the back with three contact points: back of head, upper back and sacrum. Maintain light contact during a slow hinge; do not push the head or lower back into the dowel. A fourth tactile check can be made by lightly placing the fingers at the natural lumbar curve and noticing whether its position changes substantially. Do not force the lumbar curve flat.
3. Once the pattern is controlled, practise an unloaded or very light RDL, initially through a limited range. Keep the weights close to the legs, knees slightly bent and pelvis moving backwards. Descend only as far as spinal control is maintained.
4. Increase range and load gradually, not both at once. If no external load is available, the wall drill is a learning regression, not a long-term substitute for a loaded RDL when strength or hypertrophy is the goal.

TECHNICAL CUES
- “Hips back, ribs stacked over pelvis, weights close to the legs.”
- “Keep a soft bend in the knees; knee angle changes only slightly during the descent.”
- “Lower only as far as you can maintain control; reaching the floor is not required.”
- Do not treat hamstring sensation as proof of correct technique; sensations vary. Do not diagnose from sensation.
- If the difficulty is unclear, ask one question to distinguish between spinal rounding, excessive knee bend, balance loss, limited range, grip/load difficulty, or pain.
- Keep the response concise: no more than one or two exercises at a time. Do not overwhelm the user with every possible regression.
- If the user asks for a video or visual demonstration, do not claim to have shown one unless it is actually available.
"""

